WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Vegetable oil is a liquid, and its long

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.760
molecules contain carbon to carbon double bonds.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.640
Add hydrogen gas, with a nickel catalyst and

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.440
heat, and the hydrogen adds across some of

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.760
those double bonds.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.200
The oil turns into a soft solid.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.680
For much of the last century, that's how

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.880
margarine was made.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.920
Hydrogen isn't the only thing that can add

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.120
to a double bond, though.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.680
So what can add across a C double

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.920
bond C, and what does it make?

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:31.493
Start with why.

00:00:31.774 --> 00:00:34.973
In an alkane, every carbon already has four

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:37.693
single bonds, so there's no spare bond for

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:38.493
a new atom.

00:00:38.814 --> 00:00:39.773
It's saturated.

00:00:40.254 --> 00:00:42.573
An alkene's double bond is different.

00:00:42.814 --> 00:00:44.973
As you saw in the properties video, its

00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:47.293
second bond is the weaker one, and its

00:00:47.454 --> 00:00:50.413
electrons sit above and below the carbons, where

00:00:50.494 --> 00:00:51.853
other molecules can reach them.

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.093
That second bond can break.

00:00:54.574 --> 00:00:57.373
Then each carbon has one spare bond, and

00:00:57.454 --> 00:00:59.293
a new atom can join each one.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.500
That's an addition reaction: two molecules join to

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.140
make one, and no atoms are lost.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.740
Picture the reagent, the molecule being added, splitting

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.220
in two, with one piece on each carbon

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.140
of the old double bond.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.500
Afterwards those carbons have only single bonds, so

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.460
they change from flat to tetrahedral.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.100
Alkanes have no double bond to open, so

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.180
they react in a different way, which is

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.060
the next video.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.786
Now the four reagents, one at a time.

00:01:32.027 --> 00:01:33.506
First, hydrogen.

00:01:33.787 --> 00:01:37.146
Ethene plus H two gives ethane, with one

00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:38.746
hydrogen on each carbon.

00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:41.466
The product has no double bond left, so

00:01:41.707 --> 00:01:42.586
it's saturated.

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:45.866
Hydrogen barely reacts on its own, so it

00:01:45.947 --> 00:01:48.426
needs a nickel catalyst and heat, about one

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.746
hundred and fifty degrees, and we write those

00:01:50.827 --> 00:01:52.026
conditions on the arrow.

00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:55.106
From Module five, a catalyst speeds up a

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:57.866
reaction without being used up, so it goes

00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:00.266
on the arrow, not in the equation.

00:02:00.347 --> 00:02:03.866
This is hydrogenation, the reaction that hardens vegetable

00:02:04.027 --> 00:02:04.346
oil.

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:10.126
Second, a halogen, X two, such as bromine

00:02:10.207 --> 00:02:11.166
or chlorine.

00:02:11.407 --> 00:02:14.366
Ethene plus bromine puts one bromine on each

00:02:14.447 --> 00:02:15.166
carbon.

00:02:15.407 --> 00:02:17.086
A halogen in a name works like a

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:19.326
branch: a prefix with a number.

00:02:19.727 --> 00:02:22.606
So this is one, two-dibromoethane.

00:02:23.007 --> 00:02:27.166
Chlorine works the same way, giving one, two-dichloroethane.

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:29.806
No catalyst is needed, and it's fast at

00:02:29.927 --> 00:02:30.606
room temperature.

00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:33.486
Bromine water is orange because of the bromine

00:02:33.527 --> 00:02:34.006
in it.

00:02:34.207 --> 00:02:36.606
When an alkene adds that bromine, the colour

00:02:36.687 --> 00:02:37.606
disappears.

00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:39.246
That makes it a quick test for a

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:41.126
double bond, which you'll carry out in the

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:42.206
practical video.

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:48.153
Third, a hydrogen halide, H X, such as

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:51.073
hydrogen bromide or hydrogen chloride.

00:02:51.354 --> 00:02:53.833
This time the two pieces are different: the

00:02:53.994 --> 00:02:56.473
hydrogen goes to one carbon, and the bromine

00:02:56.554 --> 00:02:57.393
to the other.

00:02:57.754 --> 00:02:59.753
Ethene gives bromoethane.

00:03:00.154 --> 00:03:02.473
It needs no number, because both carbons in

00:03:02.554 --> 00:03:03.993
ethane are alike.

00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:07.353
With hydrogen chloride, you'd get chloroethane.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.993
This one also works at room temperature, with

00:03:10.074 --> 00:03:11.033
no catalyst.

00:03:13.034 --> 00:03:14.473
Fourth, water.

00:03:14.954 --> 00:03:17.633
The pieces are H and O H.

00:03:17.914 --> 00:03:20.553
Ethene and water give ethanol, with the O

00:03:20.714 --> 00:03:22.553
H on one carbon and the H on

00:03:22.594 --> 00:03:23.033
the other.

00:03:23.594 --> 00:03:27.513
Hydration needs the most conditions: steam, an acid

00:03:27.594 --> 00:03:30.713
catalyst such as phosphoric acid, about three hundred

00:03:30.794 --> 00:03:33.353
degrees for a fast enough rate, and high

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:33.913
pressure.

00:03:34.554 --> 00:03:35.553
Why the pressure?

00:03:35.834 --> 00:03:38.473
The reaction is an equilibrium, and two gas

00:03:38.554 --> 00:03:39.913
molecules become one.

00:03:40.394 --> 00:03:43.273
From Module five, raising the pressure shifts an

00:03:43.354 --> 00:03:47.433
equilibrium towards fewer gas molecules, so towards ethanol.

00:03:47.914 --> 00:03:50.153
This is one industrial route to ethanol.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:54.566
Ethene is the same at both ends, so

00:03:54.647 --> 00:03:56.166
it gives just one product.

00:03:56.647 --> 00:03:58.046
Propene isn't.

00:03:58.327 --> 00:04:01.446
When hydrogen bromide adds to propene, the bromine

00:04:01.527 --> 00:04:03.606
can land on the end carbon, or on

00:04:03.687 --> 00:04:05.686
the middle carbon of the old double bond.

00:04:06.087 --> 00:04:09.846
So there are two possible products: one-bromopropane and

00:04:10.007 --> 00:04:11.206
two-bromopropane.

00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:13.446
In an answer, write both.

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:18.566
Water does the same, giving propan-one-ol or propan-two-ol.

00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:22.806
A symmetrical alkene, like but-two-ene, gives only one

00:04:22.887 --> 00:04:25.286
product, whichever way round the pieces add.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.500
So check every addition equation the same way.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.700
Two reactants make one product, and every atom

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.700
of the reagent ends up on the two

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:37.540
old double-bond carbons.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.900
Count them: two carbons, four hydrogens and two

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.980
bromines on each side.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:46.020
If you write HBr as a second product,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.660
you've swapped a bromine in for a hydrogen.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.220
That's substitution, not addition.

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:56.513
So, four reagents, one pattern.

00:04:56.674 --> 00:04:58.913
Split the reagent, and put one piece on

00:04:58.994 --> 00:05:02.433
each carbon: H and H, X and X,

00:05:02.514 --> 00:05:05.273
H and X, or H and O H.

00:05:05.554 --> 00:05:08.353
Then match the conditions: nickel and heat for

00:05:08.554 --> 00:05:12.433
hydrogen, room temperature for halogens and hydrogen halides,

00:05:12.514 --> 00:05:15.233
and acid, heat and pressure for water.

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:17.873
Your turn.

00:05:18.434 --> 00:05:21.313
Propene reacts with chlorine at room temperature.

00:05:21.874 --> 00:05:24.433
Write a balanced equation for this reaction using

00:05:24.514 --> 00:05:27.833
structural formulas, and name the organic product.

00:05:28.114 --> 00:05:29.393
Pause here to answer.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.320
Chlorine adds across the double bond, with one

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.720
chlorine on each carbon of the old C

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.160
double bond C.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.560
The product is C H three, C H

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.360
Cl, C H two Cl: one, two-dichloropropane.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.320
There's only one product, because both pieces are

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.440
the same.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.000
Writing H Cl as a second product would

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.440
be substitution.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.680
In an addition, nothing is lost.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:07.340
Last one.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.020
Compound X has the formula C four H

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.900
eight, and it decolourises bromine water rapidly.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.140
When X reacts with steam, using a phosphoric

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.500
acid catalyst at high temperature and pressure, only

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.060
one alcohol product is possible.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.700
Identify X, and justify your answer.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:28.700
Pause here to answer.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.420
Decolourising bromine water shows a double bond, so

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.260
X isn't a ring like cyclobutane.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.340
Three alkenes are C four H eight.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.420
But-one-ene and two-methylpropene are different at each end,

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.980
so water can add either way round, giving

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.660
two possible alcohols.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:02.220
But-two-ene is symmetrical, so it gives only butan-two-ol.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.540
So X is but-two-ene.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.100
But-one-ene doesn't fit, because it can give two

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:07.980
alcohols.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.120
So anything that splits into two pieces, H

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.560
two, X two, H X or water, can

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.360
add across a double bond.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.200
But what about alkanes, with no double bond

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.160
to open?

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.720
And alkynes, with a triple bond?

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.840
Next: why alkanes substitute, while alkenes and alkynes

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.480
add.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.940
Here are the key notes.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.100
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:36.980
to copy them.
